-- What `x.f(y)` will not accept, and why each one is a rule rather than an omission.
--
-- The dot form is `f(x, y)` for every `f` a body can name - Design.md's dot-call form - so most of
-- what can go wrong with it is what can go wrong with the call it is, and is reported by the same
-- machinery. What is collected here is the part that is about the *form*: the two places a name in
-- member position means something a name in callee position does not.

data Point {x: Int, y: Int}
data Shape = Circle {r: Int} | Square {s: Int}

fn scaled(p: Point, k: Int) -> Int = p.x * k

-- Neither half. The message names both, because both were looked for: an author who wrote this has
-- no field and no function, and either would have served.
fn noHalf(p: Point) -> Int = p.nope(1)

-- A multi-constructor receiver has no fields at all - it has to be matched on before a field of it
-- means anything - so this is the same answer with only one half ever possible.
fn overSum(s: Shape) -> Int = s.nope(1)

-- The same, on a receiver whose type is this body's own variable. A *declared* field constraint is
-- a promise that the receiver has the field, and its absence is not - which is the whole precedence
-- rule for a generic receiver, and the reason the probe is `genPropertySlot` rather than the form
-- that would report a missing constraint before the function half was ever tried.
fn overGeneric(v: a) -> Int = v.nope(1)

-- The receiver is argument 0, so the count includes it. "given 3" is what it is: the whole of what
-- the form means is that the thing to the left of the dot is an argument.
fn tooMany(p: Point) -> Int = p.scaled(1, 2)

-- Two things claiming parameter 0, which is the ordinary double-fill and is reported as one. A
-- receiver is positional by construction - it is written to the left of the name, and there is
-- nowhere to put a name on it - so there is no spelling in which this is what the author meant.
fn doubleFill(p: Point) -> Int = p.scaled(p: p, k: 2)

-- `@lazy` in position 0 is refused rather than honoured. Deciding this was a dot-call at all
-- required the receiver's *type*, which required evaluating it, and not evaluating it is exactly
-- what `@lazy` declares. The plain form is the spelling that still works, and the message says so.
fn lazyFirst(@lazy v: Int, k: Int) -> Int = v + k

fn callsLazy(n: Int) -> Int = n.lazyFirst(2)

-- A field of function type is called through the field, so a call of one that does not fit is an
-- error about the *value* rather than about any function of the name.
data Step {run: (Int) -> Int}

fn run(s: Step, a: Int, b: Int) -> Int = a + b

fn fieldArity(s: Step) -> Int = s.run(1, 2)
